<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Joseph</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Joseph of Nazareth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">canonical gospels</a>, was a Jewish man of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a> in the first century who was married to Mary and was the legal father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Matthew and Luke give him differing genealogies and infancy narratives: in Matthew an angel directs him in dreams, including the flight into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, while in Luke he travels to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a> for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. He last appears when Jesus is twelve, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a> visit to the Temple. The gospels call him a tekton, traditionally translated as carpenter, a word that can cover other craftsmen. He is venerated as a saint in the Catholic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Anglican and Lutheran traditions, and Catholics honour him as patron of workers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-joseph.jpg" alt="Saint Joseph" width="330" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guido Reni · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guido_Reni_-_St_Joseph_with_the_Infant_Jesus_-_WGA19304.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
